WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.786
Ownership and group settings are part of
the trust story for a file.

2
00:00:03.866 --> 00:00:07.092
They help define who can manage, read,
write, or replace the file,

3
00:00:07.172 --> 00:00:09.989
depending on the platform model and
surrounding policy.

4
00:00:10.069 --> 00:00:14.058
A C program that creates or consumes files
should understand which identity owns

5
00:00:14.138 --> 00:00:17.248
those files and which identities are
expected to share access.

6
00:00:17.328 --> 00:00:21.317
Shared groups and service accounts can be
useful, but they should be deliberate.

7
00:00:21.397 --> 00:00:25.152
Accidental group sharing can expose files
to services that do not need them.

8
00:00:25.232 --> 00:00:29.280
A service account that owns too much data
can turn a small application defect into a

9
00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:30.886
broader operational problem.

10
00:00:30.966 --> 00:00:33.313
The ownership model should match the
workflow,

11
00:00:33.393 --> 00:00:35.387
not merely the easiest deployment path.

12
00:00:35.467 --> 00:00:39.750
User-specific, tenant-specific, and
service-specific data need clear separation.

13
00:00:39.830 --> 00:00:43.820
Directory layout, permissions, and
application checks should all support that

14
00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:44.545
separation.

15
00:00:44.625 --> 00:00:47.266
If multiple users or tenants write into a
shared area,

16
00:00:47.346 --> 00:00:49.868
the application needs careful naming,
ownership,

17
00:00:49.948 --> 00:00:53.645
and authorization rules so one actor's
files do not become another actor's

18
00:00:53.725 --> 00:00:54.605
responsibility.

19
00:00:54.685 --> 00:00:58.264
Do not assume a file is trustworthy only
because its path looks familiar.

20
00:00:58.344 --> 00:01:01.864
A file in an expected directory may have
unexpected owner, group, type,

21
00:01:01.944 --> 00:01:03.234
permissions, or history.

22
00:01:03.314 --> 00:01:06.952
For sensitive workflows, the program may
need to check metadata or rely on

23
00:01:07.032 --> 00:01:11.080
controlled creation patterns before
trusting the file as part of the application

24
00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:11.512
state.

25
00:01:11.592 --> 00:01:14.702
Document ownership and group expectations
where they matter.

26
00:01:14.782 --> 00:01:17.891
Reviewers should be able to see which
account creates the file,

27
00:01:17.971 --> 00:01:21.081
which account reads it later, which group
if any participates,

28
00:01:21.161 --> 00:01:22.862
and where the trust boundary sits.

29
00:01:22.942 --> 00:01:25.113
Files that cross users, services, tenants,

30
00:01:25.193 --> 00:01:28.420
or privilege levels deserve especially
clear ownership design.
